{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcDfroqyseBEJ6574kVG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Dana, here is the straight answer. I can't prove nothing changed, but I found no sign of a data or schema change.\n\n**What I checked (all read-only)**\n\n- **Migrations:** still only 001, 002 and 003. 004 is not applied, so its `ALTER` and `UPDATE` have not run.\n- **audit_log:** 120,000 rows, newest entry 2026-04-07. The row count matches what I saw earlier.\n- **Database size:** still 27 MB.\n- **WAL archiving:** healthy. 6 segments archived, 0 failures, the last at 12:35 UTC today.\n\n**What I couldn't check**\n\n- **No freeze-time baseline.** I have no snapshot from when the freeze started, so I can't say whether anything changed between 6:02pm Monday and now.\n- **Postgres restarted recently.** The server started at 12:34 UTC today, about two minutes before my first check. Its statistics counters reset with it, so I can't use them to see what happened during the freeze. I don't know who or what restarted it. Ops should find out.\n- **A backup label was archived at 12:35 UTC.** That usually means a base backup ran right after the restart. I didn't start it. Ops should confirm.\n\n**Restoring to the freeze point**\n\n- The runbook says ops creates a named restore point when the freeze starts. I can't see it from here, so I can't confirm it exists.\n- If it exists and the WAL archive is complete, ops can do point-in-time recovery to it. That restores into a separate cluster, not in place, and anything legitimately done after the freeze would be lost. You would have to decide whether to keep those changes.\n- Whether a restore is possible depends on ops confirming the restore point and the archive. Whether anything needs restoring depends on what they find.\n\n**My own actions**\n\n- Every query I ran on production was a read: `SELECT`s and settings lookups. The one that tried `pg_ls_wald